  "Question": "For all positive numbers \\(x\\) distinct from \\(1,\\) \\[ \\frac1{\\log_3x}+\\frac1{\\log_4x}+\\frac1{\\log_5x} \\] equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac1{\\log_{60}x}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac1{\\log_x60}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac1{(\\log_3x)(\\log_4x)(\\log_5x)}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{12}{(\\log_3x)+(\\log_4x)+(\\log_5x)}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{\\log_2x}{(\\log_3x)(\\log_5x)}\\)\n\n\\({}+\\frac{\\log_3x}{(\\log_2x)(\\log_5x)}\\)\n\n\\({}+\\frac{\\log_5x}{(\\log_2x)(\\log_3x)}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The different bases suggest changing all three terms to one base. Since \\(x>0\\) and \\(x\\ne1\\), we can use \\(x\\) itself as that base. The change-of-base formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\log_a x}=\\log_x a\n\\qquad(a=3,4,5).\n\\]\nNow the product rule combines the sum:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{\\log_3x}+\\frac1{\\log_4x}+\\frac1{\\log_5x}\n&=\\log_x3+\\log_x4+\\log_x5\\\\\n&=\\log_x(3\\cdot4\\cdot5)\\\\\n&=\\log_x60\\\\\n&=\\frac1{\\log_{60}x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe original denominators and \\(\\log_{60}x\\) are nonzero because \\(x\\ne1\\). Thus the answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
